    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-7606",
      "problem": "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x).$ Plot the graph of $y=f\\left(2x+2\\right).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/76f9c7149797a0de54476f248f2ca0091bd585c5eb6d5a630b8262baa04e9fd6.png)",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(2x+2)=f(2(x+1))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=f\\left(2\\left(x+1\\right)\\right),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{\\left(2\\right)}=\\dfrac12$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2x\\right).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/de6bb357ea6f3b8e7e797d914759f62f4b33629644af64a4a6cd66e7a969488d.png)\n\n- Then, translate it left by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2\\left(x+1\\right)\\right).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/ba287d0600a5c0536a36e492fe25e70540f0cc7087655d7a61c190ddd42ccca9.png)",
